\title{Certified Novelty Detection for Theorem Provers:\\ A Metric Embedding Approach}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-26}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop and formally verify the metric core of a \emph{novelty certification
system} for automated mathematical research engines. Each known result is mapped
to a point of a pseudometric space $X$ --- the \textbf{theorem embedding space} ---
and the set of known results is modeled as a finite catalog $C \subseteq X$. We
define the \textbf{novelty} of a candidate output $x$ as its distance to the
nearest catalog entry, $\novelty(C, x) = \min_{c \in C} \dist(x, c)$, and a
\textbf{novelty certificate at level $\varepsilon$} as a proof of $\varepsilon \le
\novelty(C, x)$ with $\varepsilon > 0$. We prove four guarantees that justify the
slogan \emph{distance bounds novelty}: \textbf{soundness} (a positive certificate
proves $x \notin C$), \textbf{separation} (an $\varepsilon$-certificate proves $x$
is $\varepsilon$-far from every catalog entry), \textbf{stability} (novelty is
$1$-Lipschitz, so a bounded embedding error perturbs the certified novelty by at
most the same bound), and \textbf{monotonicity} (enlarging the catalog can only
decrease novelty). We give an incremental update law for streaming catalogs, a
packing/covering \textbf{novelty budget} bounding the number of mutually-novel
results in a bounded space, and --- by instantiating the abstract machinery on the
Fibonacci primitive prime divisor theorem --- an \emph{unbounded} stream of
certifiably-novel theorems. All results have been formalized and machine-checked.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Automated research engines that generate, prove, and archive mathematical
statements face a foundational quality-control problem: distinguishing genuinely
new output from rediscoveries of cataloged knowledge. Human mathematicians
adjudicate novelty by intuition; an autonomous system requires something stronger
than a heuristic label --- it requires a \emph{certificate} of novelty that is
sound, quantitative, robust to numerical error, and cheap to verify.

This paper formalizes such a certificate. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A definition of novelty as distance-to-catalog in a pseudometric
  \emph{theorem embedding space}, and of a novelty certificate as a single
  verified inequality (Section~\ref{sec:space}).
  \item \textbf{Soundness} and \textbf{separation} theorems showing a positive
  certificate proves genuine absence from the catalog with a quantitative margin
  against \emph{every} entry (Section~\ref{sec:sound}).
  \item A \textbf{$1$-Lipschitz stability} theorem --- the load-bearing robustness
  result --- making numerically computed distances into genuine certificates
  (Section~\ref{sec:stab}).
  \item \textbf{Monotonicity} and an \textbf{incremental update law} for streaming
  catalogs (Section~\ref{sec:mono}).
  \item A packing/covering \textbf{novelty budget} in bounded spaces, contrasted
  with an \textbf{unbounded novelty stream} derived from Carmichael's Fibonacci
  primitive divisor theorem (Section~\ref{sec:budget}).
\end{enumerate}

Throughout, the design choices are deliberately \emph{conservative}: we use a
pseudometric (not a metric) so that distinct results with identical embeddings are
correctly reported as non-novel, and we carry nonemptiness of the catalog
explicitly rather than assigning a junk value to the empty minimum.

\section{Preliminaries}

Let $X$ be a \textbf{pseudometric space}: a type equipped with a distance
$\dist : X \times X \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying, for all $x, y, z$,
non-negativity $\dist(x, y) \ge 0$, $\dist(x, x) = 0$, symmetry
$\dist(x, y) = \dist(y, x)$, and the triangle inequality $\dist(x, z) \le
\dist(x, y) + \dist(y, z)$. Unlike a metric, a pseudometric permits
$\dist(x, y) = 0$ for distinct $x \ne y$. This is the correct setting for a
\emph{certifier}: if two distinct theorems embed to the same point, the
conservative judgment is to declare them indistinguishable (novelty zero),
refusing to certify either as new.

A \textbf{catalog} is a finite set $C \subseteq X$. The empty catalog has no
nearest point, so novelty is only defined for nonempty $C$; we carry a
nonemptiness witness $h_C$ in every statement.

\begin{definition}[Function-valued minimum over a finite set]
\label{def:min}
For a nonempty finite set $C$ and a function $g : X \to \mathbb{R}$, the minimum
$\min_{c \in C} g(c)$ is well-defined. It satisfies (i) $\min_{c\in C} g(c) \le
g(c_0)$ for each $c_0 \in C$, and (ii) if $\ell \le g(c)$ for all $c \in C$, then
$\ell \le \min_{c \in C} g(c)$. Moreover the minimum is \emph{attained}: some
$c^\star \in C$ has $g(c^\star) = \min_{c \in C} g(c)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The embedding space and novelty certificates}
\label{sec:space}

\begin{definition}[Novelty]
For a nonempty catalog $C \subseteq X$ and a candidate $x \in X$,
\[ \novelty(C, x) \;=\; \min_{c \in C} \dist(x, c). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Novelty certificate]
A \emph{novelty certificate at level $\varepsilon$} for $x$ against $C$ is a pair
$(\varepsilon, \pi)$ with $\varepsilon > 0$ and $\pi$ a proof of $\varepsilon \le
\novelty(C, x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{novelty\_le\_dist}]
\label{lem:ledist}
For every $c \in C$, $\novelty(C, x) \le \dist(x, c)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from property (i) of the finite minimum (Definition~\ref{def:min}): the
minimum over $C$ of $c \mapsto \dist(x, c)$ is at most its value at any particular
$c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{exists\_eq\_novelty}]
\label{lem:exists}
There exists $c \in C$ with $\dist(x, c) = \novelty(C, x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By finiteness and nonemptiness, $c \mapsto \dist(x, c)$ attains its minimum on $C$
at some $c^\star$, so $\dist(x, c^\star) \le \dist(x, c)$ for all $c \in C$. By
property (ii), $\novelty(C, x) \ge \dist(x, c^\star)$; combined with
Lemma~\ref{lem:ledist} the two are equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{novelty\_nonneg}]
$0 \le \novelty(C, x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each distance is non-negative, so $0$ is a lower bound for all $\dist(x, c)$; by
property (ii) it bounds the minimum.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{le\_novelty}]
\label{lem:lenov}
If $\varepsilon \le \dist(x, c)$ for all $c \in C$, then $\varepsilon \le
\novelty(C, x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Exactly property (ii) of the finite minimum applied to the lower bound
$\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:lenov} is the \emph{certificate construction rule}: to produce an
$\varepsilon$-certificate it suffices to bound the distance to every catalog entry
from below by $\varepsilon$.

\section{Soundness and separation}
\label{sec:sound}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness, \texttt{cert\_sound}]
If $\novelty(C, x) > 0$, then $x \notin C$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Contrapositive. If $x \in C$, then by Lemma~\ref{lem:ledist} with $c = x$,
$\novelty(C, x) \le \dist(x, x) = 0$, contradicting positivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Separation, \texttt{cert\_separation}]
\label{thm:sep}
If $\varepsilon \le \novelty(C, x)$, then $\varepsilon \le \dist(x, c)$ for every
$c \in C$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $c \in C$. Chaining the hypothesis with Lemma~\ref{lem:ledist}, $\varepsilon
\le \novelty(C, x) \le \dist(x, c)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Stability: novelty is 1-Lipschitz}
\label{sec:stab}

\begin{lemma}[Additive stability, \texttt{novelty\_le\_add}]
\label{lem:add}
For all $x, y \in X$, $\novelty(C, x) \le \novelty(C, y) + \dist(x, y)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:exists} choose $c \in C$ with $\dist(y, c) = \novelty(C, y)$.
Then, using Lemma~\ref{lem:ledist} at $x$ and the triangle inequality,
\[ \novelty(C, x) \le \dist(x, c) \le \dist(x, y) + \dist(y, c)
   = \dist(x, y) + \novelty(C, y). \qedhere \]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[1-Lipschitz stability, \texttt{abs\_novelty\_sub\_le}]
\label{thm:lip}
For all $x, y \in X$, $\lvert \novelty(C, x) - \novelty(C, y) \rvert \le
\dist(x, y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:add} twice, once as stated and once with $x, y$ swapped, and
use $\dist(x, y) = \dist(y, x)$; the two inequalities bound the absolute value.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[\texttt{lipschitz\_novelty}]
The map $x \mapsto \novelty(C, x)$ is $1$-Lipschitz.
\end{corollary}

\paragraph{Robustness consequence.} If a candidate carries an $\varepsilon$-certificate
computed from an embedding $\tilde{x}$ with $\dist(x, \tilde{x}) \le \delta$, then
by Theorem~\ref{thm:lip}, $\novelty(C, x) \ge \novelty(C, \tilde{x}) - \delta \ge
\varepsilon - \delta$. Whenever $\varepsilon > \delta$ the certificate survives:
errors pass through novelty with gain at most $1$.

\section{Monotonicity and streaming updates}
\label{sec:mono}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity, \texttt{novelty\_mono}]
If $C \subseteq D$ (both nonempty), then $\novelty(D, x) \le \novelty(C, x)$ for
all $x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:exists} choose $c \in C$ realizing $\novelty(C, x) = \dist(x,
c)$. Since $C \subseteq D$, $c \in D$, so $\novelty(D, x) \le \dist(x, c) =
\novelty(C, x)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:ledist} applied to $D$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Incremental update, \texttt{novelty\_insert}]
For $a \in X$ and nonempty $C$,
\[ \novelty(C \cup \{a\}, x) = \min\bigl(\dist(x, a),\, \novelty(C, x)\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The minimum of $c \mapsto \dist(x, c)$ over $C \cup \{a\}$ splits as the minimum of
the value at $a$ and the minimum over $C$; this is the standard recursion under
insertion, and holds whether or not $a \in C$.
\end{proof}

This gives an $O(1)$ streaming update: a new theorem refreshes any candidate's
novelty with one distance computation and one comparison.

\section{Novelty budget vs.\ an unbounded novelty stream}
\label{sec:budget}

Call a catalog $\varepsilon$-\textbf{separated} if any two distinct entries are at
distance at least $\varepsilon$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}, such a catalog is a
collection of mutually-novel results, each carrying an $\varepsilon$-certificate
against all the others.

\begin{proposition}[Novelty budget, packing bound]
\label{prop:budget}
If the embedding space is covered by $N$ cells each of diameter $< \varepsilon$,
then any $\varepsilon$-separated catalog has at most $N$ entries.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Two points in the same cell are at distance $< \varepsilon$, so an
$\varepsilon$-separated set contains at most one point per cell.
\end{proof}

If the embedding lands in a $d$-dimensional box of side $R$, dividing into cells of
side $\varepsilon/\sqrt{d}$ gives $N = O((R\sqrt{d}/\varepsilon)^d)$ cells, so the
maximum number of mutually $\varepsilon$-novel theorems is $O((R/\varepsilon)^d)$:
in a bounded space, novelty is a finite, rationed resource.

\medskip

The budget vanishes when the space is unbounded. Recall the Fibonacci numbers
$F_1 = F_2 = 1$, $F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}$. A prime $q$ is a \textbf{primitive
prime divisor} of $F_n$ if $q \mid F_n$ but $q \nmid F_k$ for all $0 < k < n$.

\begin{theorem}[Carmichael, prime-index case; \texttt{fib\_prime\_index\_has\_primitive}]
\label{thm:carmichael}
For every prime $p \ge 3$, $F_p$ has a primitive prime divisor.
\end{theorem}

Let $\carPrime(p)$ be a chosen primitive prime of $F_p$, and embed prime indices by
$\carEmbed(p) = \carPrime(p) \in \mathbb{R}$.

\begin{lemma}[Distinctness]
\label{lem:distinct}
For distinct primes $p, p' \ge 3$, $\carPrime(p) \ne \carPrime(p')$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
WLOG $p < p'$. If $q = \carPrime(p) = \carPrime(p')$, then as the primitive prime
of $F_{p'}$, $q$ divides no $F_k$ with $0 < k < p'$; but $q$ divides $F_p$ with
$0 < p < p'$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded novelty budget]
The catalog $\{\carEmbed(p) : p \ge 3 \text{ prime}\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is
$1$-separated and infinite. Hence it contains arbitrarily large finite
sub-catalogs in which every member carries a novelty certificate at level $1$
against all others.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:distinct} the embedded values are distinct integers, so any two
differ by at least $1$ ($1$-separation), and the map is injective on the infinite
set of primes $p \ge 3$, so the image is infinite. For any finite sub-catalog,
Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} turns $1$-separation into a level-$1$ certificate per member.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the exact counterpoint to Proposition~\ref{prop:budget}: the packing bound
caps mutually-novel results in any bounded region, while the unbounded prime line,
populated by Carmichael's theorem, hosts an endless $1$-separated catalog. The
construction uses primitivity only through the abstract ``divides no earlier term''
clause, which holds for every non-degenerate Lucas sequence.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

Let $n = |C|$ and embeddings live in $\mathbb{R}^d$. \emph{Novelty evaluation}:
compute all $\dist(x, c)$ and take the minimum, $O(nd)$. \emph{Certificate
verification}: verify $\varepsilon \le \dist(x, c)$ for all $c$, rejecting on the
first violation, $O(nd)$. \emph{Streaming update}: $\novelty(C, x) \leftarrow
\min(\dist(x, a), \novelty(C, x))$, $O(d)$ per candidate. \emph{Budget estimate}:
count occupied grid cells of side $\varepsilon/\sqrt{d}$.

\section{Applications and Discussion}

The framework supports quality control for autonomous research engines (report
only above-threshold certificates, with a verifiable margin), robust deduplication
under learned embeddings (certificates survive whenever the margin exceeds the
model's embedding error, by Theorem~\ref{thm:lip}), and budget planning (the
packing bound estimates novel yield of bounded regions; the Fibonacci stream marks
inexhaustible ones). The same template applies to plagiarism detection, patent
prior-art search, and de-novo molecular design.

The mathematical backbone is the classical fact that distance-to-a-set is
$1$-Lipschitz, specialized to a finite set. What is new is the \emph{certification
framing}: soundness, separation, stability, and monotonicity as the contract a
novelty detector must satisfy, each a machine-checked theorem, so that a
numerically computed distance becomes a formal guarantee. Two conservative choices
keep the certifier honest: the pseudometric setting (coincident embeddings $\Rightarrow$
novelty zero) and the explicit nonemptiness witness (no junk value for the empty
minimum).

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp novelty budget via Lipschitz embeddings]
If $f : X \to \mathbb{R}^d$ is an $L$-bi-Lipschitz embedding with image in a box of
side $R$, the maximum size of an $\varepsilon$-separated catalog is
$\Theta((RL/\varepsilon)^d)$, matching the packing bound up to the doubling
constant. The upper half holds with no geometric assumption; only an explicit
$\varepsilon$-net lower-bound construction remains.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Novelty as the Hausdorff gap of catalog growth]
For nested catalogs $C_0 \subseteq C_1 \subseteq \cdots$, the certified novelties
$\novelty(C_n, x)$ are non-increasing and converge to $\dist(x, \overline{\bigcup_n
C_n})$; moreover $\sup_x |\novelty(C_n, x) - \novelty(C_m, x)| = d_H(C_n, C_m)$,
the Hausdorff distance. Monotonicity is established; the remaining step is the
identification with $d_H$ via the $1$-Lipschitz law.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Every Lucas sequence yields an unbounded novelty stream]
For any non-degenerate Lucas sequence $U_n(P, Q)$ (Fibonacci, Pell, Mersenne,
\dots), the primitive-prime map induces a $1$-separated real catalog of unbounded
size. The Fibonacci construction used primitivity only through the abstract
``divides no earlier term'' clause; Carmichael's theorem holds for all
non-degenerate Lucas sequences, so the construction is parametric over the same
rank-of-apparition spine.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

We have given a formally verified foundation for certified novelty detection:
novelty as distance-to-catalog in a pseudometric embedding space, certificates as
single verified inequalities, and four guarantees --- soundness, separation,
$1$-Lipschitz stability, and monotonicity --- that make those certificates
trustworthy under numerical error and catalog growth. A packing-based novelty
budget quantifies the scarcity of novelty in bounded spaces, while Carmichael's
Fibonacci primitive divisor theorem furnishes an explicit, unbounded stream of
certifiably-novel results. Distance, it turns out, really does bound novelty ---
and in the right space, it bounds it from below forever.

